// SOS M3 unit 6: THE USERSPACE UART ECHO DRIVER, AS A CHILD (sawos design 9
// D-2; spec §2.5, §9, §12). The board whose console is an NS16550A.
//
// **THE PROMISE IN `uart-echo-ns16550`'s HEADER, KEPT.** That file — the M2
// money shot, migrated in M3 unit 4 to obtain its window instead of declaring it
// — ends with a sentence about what the migration bought:
//
//     a window that arrives as a capability is a window somebody can HAND OVER.
//     The M2 shape could only ever grant the one process the kernel loads, which
//     is why a child image was refused a device window outright; unit 6's driver
//     child is what this makes possible.
//
// This is that child. It is not root: it was created by a launcher
// (`driver-child` beside it), it received its authority in its first argument
// register like every SOS process does (§12's symmetry), and the console's
// register page reached it as an `IoMemory` a launcher GAVE it under a tag.
//
// **THE DRIVER BODY BELOW IS THE ROOT-AS-DRIVER TWIN'S, UNCHANGED** — same
// register block, same addresses, same enable bit, same drain loop, same
// console lines, same line number in the last one. THREE THINGS DIFFER, all of
// them about being a child rather than about being a driver:
//
//   1. the window arrives by GIVE rather than as a boot region row. The drain
//      call is byte-identical (`boot_handle_next` + `take_iomemory`), which is
//      the point: a record is a record, and whether the kernel or a launcher
//      queued it is not something the receiver can tell or needs to.
//   2. it ends with `Process.exit`, not `System.shutdown`. Its System handle is
//      a MASKED sibling of root's — `Debug | ProcessSelf | ClockGet | Transfer`
//      — so stopping the machine is not a thing it can spell. A driver that
//      could halt the box on a bad byte would be a worse driver.
//   3. it is linked at the CHILD base (`hal/<arch>/user/child.ld`), because on a
//      machine with no translation two processes cannot both live at root's
//      address.
//
// EVERYTHING ELSE IS THE SAME PROGRAM, and that is the claim. The kernel's whole
// part is still three things — it routed the line and woke this thread, and it
// did not even grant the window this time; a PROCESS did.
//
// THE CYCLE, which is spec §9 read from the driver's side:
//
//   0. obtain the window      drain the boot set for the `IoMemory` its launcher
//                             gave it and `Process.map(iomemory:)` it into this
//                             process
//   1. bind the line          `Process.interrupt_bind(line:)`
//   2. watch it               `Waiter.add(&interrupt, key)`
//   3. enable the DEVICE      write its own interrupt-enable register — the
//                             kernel never touches it, because the register is
//                             behind the window and the window is this
//                             process's
//   4. park                   `Waiter.wait()`, which answers with the key and
//                             the line
//   5. service                drain the receiver and echo each byte, ALL of it
//                             before the ack (§9's release rule)
//   6. `interrupt.ack()`      unmask; a device still asserting fires again
//
// WHY THIS FILE NAMES A DEVICE when every other SOS process is arch-free: a
// driver IS its device. The two profiles have different UARTs, so there are two
// of these packages, named for the CHIP rather than for the machine — which is
// the honest axis, since the same 16550 would want the same file on a different
// architecture. Its launcher names no device at all.
//
// THE ECHOED BYTES ARE THE TEST. The harness feeds a known short string into
// the emulator's serial input and asserts it comes back out AFTER the kernel's
// handover marker — which now means it came out of a process that root created,
// through a device root gave away.

import sos.{System, Process, Interrupt, Mapping, Waiter, WaitResult,
            WaitPayload}

// The scalar-to-byte rule, shared with the kernel's own console so a banner
// written through the granted window and one written through a System op agree
// about what a non-ASCII scalar becomes.
import sosrt.{ascii_byte}

// =============================================================================
// The board
// =============================================================================

/// The console UART's register page — where the window this driver is HANDED
/// lives, and the same number the kernel's board description carries.
///
/// **THE NUMBER DID NOT MOVE WHEN THE GIVER DID** (sawos design 9 D-2), which is
/// the same statement design 6 made about the ACQUISITION and is worth making
/// once more here: SOS does not translate (spec §5.5), so a mapped window is
/// where the hardware is, whoever handed it over. In M2 this address was in a
/// manifest; in M3 unit 4 it was a boot region row; here it arrives from another
/// PROCESS. The driver's constant is unchanged in all three, because a driver
/// knowing its own device's address is a fact about the device.
static UART_BASE: UInt = 0x1000_0000

/// The interrupt line this board wires the console to.
static UART_IRQ: UInt = 10

/// The key this driver attaches under. It is ITS OWN WORD — the kernel stores
/// one and hands it back unread — so a dispatcher watching several devices
/// tells them apart by it. Here there is one, and the wait answer is checked
/// against it anyway, because a key that came back wrong is the one bug this
/// program could not otherwise see.
static ECHO_KEY: UInt = 0x5541

/// How many bytes the driver echoes before it stops. The harness feeds exactly
/// this many; a byte lost anywhere leaves this loop parked and the case fails on
/// the emulator's timeout rather than passing quietly.
static ECHO_BYTES: Int = 4

static EXIT_OK: UInt = 0
static EXIT_FAILED: UInt = 1

// =============================================================================
// The NS16550A, through the granted window (design 112's blessed MMIO idiom)
// =============================================================================

/// The register block. Registers are byte-spaced; declaration order IS the
/// layout (design 58), so each field sits at its hardware offset. `ReadOnly<T>`
/// exposes only `.read()`.
struct UartRegs {
    thr: UInt8,             // +0  transmit holding / receive buffer
    ier: UInt8,             // +1  interrupt enable
    iir: UInt8,             // +2  interrupt id / FIFO control
    lcr: UInt8,             // +3  line control
    mcr: UInt8,             // +4  modem control
    lsr: ReadOnly<UInt8>,   // +5  line status
    msr: ReadOnly<UInt8>,   // +6  modem status
    scr: UInt8,             // +7  scratch
}

static_assert(sizeof<UartRegs>() == 8,
              "the 16550 register block is eight byte-wide registers")

/// IER bit 0: interrupt when the receiver holds a byte. This is the ONE bit
/// that makes the device assert its line, and writing it is the driver's — the
/// kernel unmasked the line at the controller and stopped there.
static IER_RX_READY: UInt8 = 0x01

/// LSR bit 0: a byte is waiting. LSR bit 5: the transmitter will take one.
static LSR_DATA_READY: UInt8 = 0x01
static LSR_TX_EMPTY: UInt8 = 0x20

/// The driver. A SAFE type (unsafety is not transitive — design 130): only the
/// methods reaching through `regs` carry the `unsafe` effect.
struct Uart {
    regs: UnsafeMemory<UartRegs, Device>,
}

extension Uart {
    init(at: UInt) unsafe -> Uart {
        Uart(regs: UnsafeMemory<UartRegs, Device>(at))
    }

    /// Let the device raise its line when a byte arrives.
    func listen(&self) unsafe {
        self.regs.ier.write(IER_RX_READY)
    }

    func has_byte(&self) unsafe -> Bool {
        (self.regs.lsr.read() & LSR_DATA_READY) != 0
    }

    /// Take the waiting byte. Reading the register is also what CLEARS the
    /// device's assertion, which is why nothing else has to.
    func take_byte(&self) unsafe -> UInt8 {
        self.regs.thr.read()
    }

    func put_byte(&self, b: UInt8) unsafe {
        while (self.regs.lsr.read() & LSR_TX_EMPTY) == 0 { }
        self.regs.thr.write(b)
    }

    func put_str(&self, msg: String) unsafe {
        for scalar in msg.chars() {
            self.put_byte(ascii_byte(scalar))
        }
    }

    /// Drain everything the receiver holds, echoing each byte, and report how
    /// many. A LOOP rather than one byte per interrupt: the line is level
    /// -triggered either way, so both work, but draining is what a driver
    /// should do — it turns a burst into one wake instead of one wake per byte.
    func echo_available(&self) unsafe -> Int {
        var n = 0
        while self.has_byte() {
            self.put_byte(self.take_byte())
            n = n + 1
        }
        n
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// The driver
// =============================================================================

/// The line a wait answered with. An Event answer would mean the kernel
/// reported an attachment this process never made, which is a bug in the pair
/// rather than a condition to handle.
func woke_on_line(r: &WaitResult) -> UInt {
    match r.what {
        case Interrupt(line) -> line,
        case Event(_) -> panic("this driver attached no event"),
        case Timer(_) -> panic("this driver attached no timer"),
        case Process(_) -> panic("this driver attached no process"),
    }
}

@export("_start")
func _start(boot_handle: UInt) {
    let system = System(boot_handle: boot_handle)

    // §12's SYMMETRY, and it is what makes the rest of this file the same
    // program root ran: a child bootstraps exactly as root does — System in the
    // first argument register, its own Process handle derived from it. The
    // handle is a MASKED sibling of its launcher's, and nothing below can tell.
    //
    // A failure here cannot report through `exit`, because `exit` is the op on
    // the handle that just failed to arrive. It returns instead, which ends this
    // process the way a child with no authority always ends.
    let proc = try system.process_self() catch {
        print("SOS echo: no process handle: {}\n", error)
        return
    }

    // **STEP 0 — OBTAIN THE WINDOW**, and the drain is the twin's drain
    // unchanged. What differs is upstream of this call and invisible to it: the
    // record was queued by `ProcessOp.Give` from a launcher rather than by the
    // kernel out of a DEVICE row of the build's region table. A record is a
    // record; the tag is the giver's word and this driver never reads it,
    // because its launcher's config gave it exactly one.
    //
    // THE MAPPING IS HELD FOR THE PROGRAM'S LIFE. Dropping it would release the
    // HANDLE and not the row (§2.5: a mapping dropped without an unmap is
    // permanent), so the registers would stay reachable either way — but a
    // driver that means to keep its device keeps the capability that names the
    // mapping, which is what `unmap` would need.
    var record = try proc.boot_handle_next() catch {
        print("SOS echo: no device window: {}\n", error)
        let _ = proc.exit(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    guard let window = record.take_iomemory() else {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS echo: boot record is not a window\n")
        let _ = proc.exit(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    // **AND THE CHILD MAPS IT INTO ITSELF**, which is the handoff's ruled lean:
    // what crossed the process boundary was authority over a device, and where
    // the row goes is the holder's own business. It spends `IoMemoryRight.Map`
    // on the window its launcher moved here and `ProcessRight.Map` on this
    // process's own handle — two rights on two objects, neither of them root's.
    let _mapping = try proc.map(iomemory: &window) catch {
        print("SOS echo: map failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = proc.exit(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS echo: window mapped\n")

    // §2's Interrupt row: the line becomes an object, and the object is
    // waitable. The kernel unmasks it here — the DEVICE is still quiet, because
    // its own enable bit is below. It spends `ProcessRight.InterruptBind`, which
    // arrived on the handle `ProcessSelf` minted and is therefore NOT something
    // this driver's launcher granted; see `driver-child`'s recorded finding.
    let irq = try proc.interrupt_bind(line: UART_IRQ) catch {
        print("SOS echo: no interrupt: {}\n", error)
        let _ = proc.exit(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let waiter = try proc.waiter_create() catch {
        print("SOS echo: no waiter: {}\n", error)
        let _ = proc.exit(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    match waiter.add(&irq, ECHO_KEY) {
        case Ok(_) -> {},
        case Err(e) -> {
            print("SOS echo: attach failed: {}\n", e)
            let _ = proc.exit(EXIT_FAILED)
            return
        },
    }

    // From here on every console byte goes out through the GRANTED WINDOW, not
    // through a System op — which is the point of the grant and is what the
    // handover protocol makes safe to do.
    let uart = Uart(at: UART_BASE)
    uart.listen()
    uart.put_str("SOS echo: driver up\n")

    var echoed = 0
    var line: UInt = 0        // a line number, which is what `woke_on_line` answers
    var wakes = 0
    while echoed < ECHO_BYTES {
        let answer = try waiter.wait() catch {
            print("SOS echo: wait failed: {}\n", error)
            let _ = proc.exit(EXIT_FAILED)
            return
        }
        line = woke_on_line(&answer)
        wakes = wakes + 1
        if answer.key != ECHO_KEY {
            print("SOS echo: woke on key {}, not {}\n", answer.key, ECHO_KEY)
            let _ = proc.exit(EXIT_FAILED)
            return
        }

        // EVERYTHING THAT TOUCHES THE DEVICE HAPPENS BEFORE THE ACK — spec §9's
        // release rule. After the ack the line is live again and a second fire
        // may be delivered, so a register read moved below it would be racing
        // the next byte.
        echoed = echoed + uart.echo_available()
        match irq.ack() {
            case Ok(_) -> {},
            case Err(e) -> {
                print("SOS echo: ack failed: {}\n", e)
                let _ = proc.exit(EXIT_FAILED)
                return
            },
        }
    }

    // The last line goes through the RUNTIME SEAM instead, which is the other
    // altitude and is still the process's own sequencing — the kernel writes
    // because it was asked to.
    // THE WAKE COUNT IS REPORTED AND NOT ASSERTED. Every byte is echoed and at
    // least one interrupt was serviced — that is what the line number proves,
    // since it came out of the wait RECORD — but how many wakes it took depends
    // on how fast the host feeds the port relative to the guest, so pinning a
    // number would be pinning the emulator's timing.
    print("\nSOS echo: done {} bytes on line {} after {} wakes\n",
          echoed, line, wakes)

    // **AND IT EXITS RATHER THAN STOPPING THE MACHINE**, which is the one line
    // of the cycle a child could not have written the twin's way: this driver
    // holds no `SystemRight.Shutdown`, so the op does not exist for it. Its
    // launcher is parked on this process's death and takes the machine down
    // afterwards, which is where that decision belongs.
    let _ = proc.exit(EXIT_OK)
}
